{
  "note": "Practice mock data: the known sentence the chunk responses were synthesized from.",
  "text": "Welcome back to the workshop. Today we are building a small weather station for the roof. First we need a sensor that reads the temperature every ten seconds. The sensor is cheap, so we will buy two and compare them. We measure, we log, we measure, we log, and then we compare the numbers. If the numbers disagree by more than a degree, one sensor is wrong. Next comes the wind gauge, which is just a spinning cup on a short pole. Every turn of the cup closes a switch and the board counts the turns. More turns per minute means more wind, and we turn that into meters per second. Keep the pole away from the chimney, because warm air from the chimney spoils the readings. Now let us talk about power. A small solar panel and one battery can run the whole station for weeks. On cloudy days the battery carries the load, and on sunny days the panel fills it again. Check the battery voltage once a day for the first week. Very important, very important, never mount the board without a plastic cover. Rain finds every gap, so seal the cable hole with soft rubber. The board sends one message every five minutes to the home network. Each message holds the temperature, the wind speed and the battery level. On the receiving side a tiny program stores every message in a plain text file. A plain text file is easy to read, easy to copy and easy to back up. After a month we draw a chart of the temperature for every single day. The chart shows warm afternoons, cool nights and one very windy weekend. Then we compare the chart with the forecast from the local weather service. Sometimes the forecast wins and sometimes our small station wins. We measure, we log, we measure, we log, and that habit is the whole project. Thank you for following along, and see you in the next workshop.",
